# Run Only the Tests That Failed Last Time

Since 3.6.2, `runLastFailed` lets you run again only the tests that went wrong in the previous build.
It helps with long test suites: a few tests failed, you fixed the code (or restarted that flaky external service),
and you don't want to run everything again just to check them.

```bash
mvn ${goal}
# a few tests fail, you fix them
mvn -D${thisPlugin.toLowerCase()}.runLastFailed ${goal}
```

The previous build is read from the XML reports in `reportsDirectory`, so **don't run `clean` in between**.

## What runs

- Test methods that ended with a failure or an error. Tests that passed, were skipped or were flaky
  (they failed but passed on a rerun) don't run again.
- Whole test classes that didn't finish because the previous build stopped early: a crashed or killed forked JVM,
  a `forkedProcessTimeoutInSeconds` timeout, or Maven itself being stopped. Tests that never had a chance to run
  get their turn.

If the previous build had no failures, no tests run and the plugin says so. If there are no reports yet,
for instance right after `clean`, all tests run as usual.

The `test` parameter, `includes` and `excludes` still apply. With `-Dtest=MyTest`, only the tests of `MyTest`
that failed last time run.

## Not the same as rerunFailingTestsCount

[`rerunFailingTestsCount`](./rerun-failing-tests.html) retries a failing test straight away, within the same build,
to deal with flaky tests. `runLastFailed` works across builds: it remembers what went wrong last time.
You can use both together.

## Good to know

- After such a build, the report of each class that ran again only lists the tests that ran again.
  The reports of the other classes are left untouched.
- All the invocations of a parameterized test run again, not only the ones that failed.
- A test class that failed outside a test method (e.g. in a `@BeforeAll` method) runs again in full.
  So does a class whose failed test names can't be mapped back to a Java method, e.g. with
  `usePhrasedTestCaseMethodName` in the `statelessTestsetReporter` configuration.
- With `usePhrasedTestCaseClassName`, the reports don't hold the class names any more, so all tests run.
- To know whether the previous build finished, the plugin keeps a small hidden file next to `reportsDirectory`
  while the tests run, e.g. `target/.${thisPlugin.toLowerCase()}-reports-run-in-progress`.
- A `reportsDirectory` containing `${esc.d}{surefire.forkNumber}` isn't supported, all tests run.
